TODO
====

input

- synthetic release on window blur/occlusion. a drag whose release is lost
  to a mid-gesture visibility change leaves glass drag state (left or
  right button) dangling. a synthetic pointer-up at the last known position
  closes both. glass's drop-is-authoritative contract absorbs it cleanly.
  native half is a GLFW focus callback, browser half blur/visibilitychange.

protocol and platform

- resize should report tree space, not window space. with an in-window
  menubar the tree's layout space is smaller than the window by one row
  height, so fill-derived sizes are wrong server-side. report the space the
  tree lays out in, and re-report when a later menu op adds or removes the
  bar.
- video node. trivial in the browser (hidden video element into
  texImage2D), but a codec dependency natively. options: browser-only and
  accept the split, take the ffmpeg dependency and keep parity, or stay
  out and let apps hand frames in as images. decide when an app needs it.
- color emoji. needs a COLR paint-graph interpreter over go-text's exposed
  paint data (the color face is 4.8MB vs 1.9MB monochrome). wait for
  demand.
- binary wire framing (msgpack or flatbuffers). if ever JSON measurably
  hurts.

auth

the model: auth is app state, not framework machinery. mount a login tree,
check credentials in the handler, SetRoot to the real app. Authorize is for
apps that already have SSO or a proxy in front and want the socket refused
before it opens.

- client storage op. this is the gap that makes app-level auth incomplete:
  after a page reload the app gets a fresh mount with no way to know this
  browser was logged in a second ago, so the user signs in again. the only
  thing the client stores today is the resume token in sessionStorage, good
  for the 60s grace and nothing else. add a generic key/value op: the server
  says remember this, the client keeps it in localStorage and presents what
  it has at mount. the app issues its own token after login and checks it on
  the next mount. not auth specific (ui prefs want the same thing), roughly
  thirty lines on each side.
- resumeGrace is a hardcoded 60s const in session.go. make it an option.
- no way to end a session from outside. add s.Close() for when the app
  decides a session is over.
- document that sessions are a sync.Map in one process, so a deployment
  behind a load balancer needs sticky routing. optionally sign resume
  tokens so another instance can tell "not mine" from "expired" instead of
  behaving oddly.
- moving a live session between instances is not possible in the go sdk and
  probably never will be: a session is a closure graph.
  the recovery path is remount, with the app rebuilding from its own store. the
  clojure sdk is the one that could actually do it, since state is
  immutable data and the view is a pure function of it.
- what someone would actually build instead is affinity: find the instance
  already serving them and send them there. the bounce belongs on the host
  page request, not the socket, since browsers do not reliably follow
  redirects on a websocket handshake but the page load is ordinary http.
  the framework's only job is to not foreclose it: the resume token is an
  opaque random string today, and if an instance id were in it (or an app
  could supply a prefix) a load balancer could route on the query param
  alone. worth remembering whenever tokens get signed.
- if an app does use Authorize: a rejected connection currently looks like
  an outage. the 403 on the upgrade reaches the terminals as a connection
  error they retry forever, so a signed-out user sees a network failure.
  and gating the host page means owning "/", which ServeClient registers
  along with /app.js and /shaper.wasm, so it needs splitting up before an
  app can put its own check in front.

shipping

- codesigning and notarization for distributing .apps beyond the local
  machine.
- wayland desktop pass on real hardware. checklist in linux/README.md
  first item is the missing xdg_toplevel app_id (needs GLFW 3.4's
  GLFW_WAYLAND_APP_ID).
